---
import Product from '../../layouts/Product.astro';
import PersonHouseholdRecords from '../../components/PersonHouseholdRecords.astro';
import { products } from '../../data/products';
const product = products.find((p) => p.key === 'base-registry')!;
---
<Product
  product={product}
  heading="Store and manage the records your services rely on."
  description="Base Registry Engine holds the register your organization is responsible for: the records, who may change them, and the history of every change. Other applications create, find, and update records through it under your rules. A base registry, called a basisregistratie in the Netherlands and an authentic source in Belgium, is the agreed source for defined information about people, organizations, places, or assets."
>
  <section id="product-example" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="registry-lifecycle-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">From the first record to years of changes</p>
        <h2 id="registry-lifecycle-title">Create, connect, and maintain your records.</h2>
        <p>A local authority could use the engine for a register of residents and households. Staff register people, find existing records, and update details as circumstances change. The engine stores the information and its history; your application provides the screens.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="record-example" aria-labelledby="record-example-caption">
        <div class="stored-records">
          <p class="record-owner">Stored in Base Registry Engine</p>
          <PersonHouseholdRecords />
          <p class="record-storage">Two linked records, each with its own fields and change history.</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="record-lifecycle">
          <li><h3>Register and connect</h3><p>Create a person’s record and link it to a household. The engine checks required information, permitted values, and references before saving it.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Find and use</h3><p>Look up a resident or retrieve the people linked to a household. Each application can access the records and fields its permissions allow.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Keep up with changes</h3><p>Correct a detail or update a household relationship. The engine stores the current information and preserves earlier revisions so authorized users can follow what changed.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <figcaption id="record-example-caption">Illustrative register with fictional records. The same approach can link a business to its establishments, a professional to licences, or an asset to its location.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="breg-records-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Your records, your rules</p>
        <h2 id="breg-records-title">A shared foundation for your applications.</h2>
        <p>Define the register once, then use it across the applications that maintain and consult it. The engine applies the same configured rules to each request.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-facts">
        <article><h3>Model the information you need</h3><p>Define record types, required fields, valid values, and relationships. Your register can hold several kinds of records together, such as people, households, and memberships.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Control access and changes</h3><p>Decide which records and fields each role can read or change. Routine updates can be applied directly. When an Action requires review, the engine holds the proposed change and accepts a matching result from the configured external review authority. An authorized caller then applies an approved proposal. Related changes can be applied together so they stay consistent.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Keep continuity over time</h3><p>Inspect earlier revisions and prevent an older edit from overwriting a newer change. As your needs evolve, update the record definitions through versioned configuration and planned data migrations.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="breg-duties-title">
    <div class="section-inner product-fit">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">Connect the capabilities your service needs</p>
        <h2 id="breg-duties-title">Keep record management separate from review and information sharing.</h2>
        <p>Base Registry Engine manages records and permitted changes. Evidence Gateway and Registry Casework have separate responsibilities and run independently. Add and connect them only when the service needs those capabilities.</p>
      </div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Manage the source records</dt><dd>Base Registry Engine stores the records and their history and applies your rules for who may read or change them.</dd>
        <dt>Provide a limited answer when a full record is unnecessary</dt><dd><a href="/products/evidence-gateway/">Evidence Gateway</a> can return a defined, digitally signed answer, such as an age check, without returning the full record. A service that needs records reads them through the access your organization grants.</dd>
        <dt>Coordinate work that needs a person to decide</dt><dd><a href="/products/casework/">Registry Casework</a> owns the review policy and decision. Base Registry Engine holds the proposal, accepts only a result that matches it, and still requires an authorized caller to apply an approved change.</dd>
        <dt>Review changes and refused requests</dt><dd>The engine records changes and refused requests made by authenticated callers, so an authorized reviewer can reconstruct what happened and which identity made the request. A request without a verified identity is rejected before it can add an entry to the registry audit journal.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="breg-fit-title">
    <div class="section-inner product-fit">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it fits your organization</p>
        <h2 id="breg-fit-title">Build your services around a register you control.</h2>
        <p>Your organization owns the records and runs the engine and its database on infrastructure you control. Connect staff applications, public services, and other systems through the engine’s interface.</p>
        <p>Use it to build a new register or move an existing register into a defined structure, with a planned migration of its records.</p>
      </div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Your team defines the register</dt><dd>Agree what the records mean, how they relate, and who may use them. Your technical team translates those decisions into the configuration that defines storage, validation, and access.</dd>
        <dt>Your applications serve the people using it</dt><dd>Your team or delivery partner provides the screens, sign-in, and wider service workflows. The engine handles record storage and operations behind those applications.</dd>
        <dt>Add the other products when needed</dt><dd>Each product runs on its own. Connect Evidence Gateway or Casework to the register when another service needs a fact or a person’s decision, and not before.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="developers" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="breg-developers-title">
    <div class="section-inner product-fit">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">For developers and implementers</p>
        <h2 id="breg-developers-title">Create your first register and work with its records.</h2>
        <p>The introductory tutorial creates a local contact registry. Add and read one contact, then confirm that a test caller from another jurisdiction cannot list it.</p>
        <p><a class="inline-cta" href={product.tutorialHref}>Run the introductory tutorial →</a></p>
        <p>To configure external review, pair a registry Proposal Action with a Casework review policy and bind Casework as the registry’s review authority. The tutorial checks that the two projects agree; it does not run a full approval and application journey.</p>
        <p><a class="inline-cta" href="https://docs.registrystack.org/tutorials/review-breg-changes-in-casework/">Pair a registry proposal with Casework →</a></p>
      </div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Database and application interface</dt><dd>A versioned model defines the PostgreSQL structure, validation, REST API, and OpenAPI description. Existing records require an explicit migration into the engine.</dd>
        <dt>Changes that belong together</dt><dd>Configured actions can create or update several records in one transaction. Changes to the model are reviewed and tested, with a database migration where the structure changes.</dd>
        <dt>Continue in the documentation</dt><dd><a href="https://docs.registrystack.org/explanation/configuration-defined-registry/">Read how a configuration-defined registry works</a> for the model, permissions, and application boundary. The tutorials provide their prerequisites and executable steps.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
</Product>

<style>
  .record-example { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.85fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 24px 40px; align-items: start; margin: 0; }
  .stored-records { min-width: 0; background: var(--registry-slate-tint); border: 1px solid var(--registry-slate-rule); padding: clamp(16px, 2vw, 24px); }
  .record-owner { color: var(--registry-blue); font-size: var(--type-sm); font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .record-storage { color: var(--registry-body); font-size: var(--type-xs); line-height: 1.6; margin: 16px 0 0; }
  .record-lifecycle { display: grid; gap: 22px; margin: 0; padding-left: 26px; }
  .record-lifecycle li { padding-left: 10px; }
  .record-lifecycle li::marker { color: var(--registry-blue); font-family: var(--registry-mono); font-weight: 600; }
  .record-lifecycle h3 { font-size: var(--type-subhead); margin: 0 0 6px; }
  .record-lifecycle p { margin: 0; }
  .record-example figcaption { grid-column: 1 / -1; color: var(--registry-muted); font-size: var(--type-base); line-height: 1.5; }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .record-example { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
